#pragma once

// The one way JusPrin reports an unexpected error (AGENTS.md, Error
// handling): a failure no person decided an outcome for travels up unchanged
// to the handler at the top of its entry point, and that handler, and only
// it, calls report_unexpected_error. The person is told "Something went wrong
// on our side" in ErrorReportDialog and nothing more specific; the report
// goes straight to the app's Sentry project (SentryClient.hpp) once they
// press "Report to JusPrin team" or after they turned automatic reports on.
//
// The top handlers today: the Agent and Home pages' message handlers
// (AgentWebView, HomeWebView), the pumps that read an AI reply or title and a
// key check (AgentHost::pump_stream, AgentHost::pump_setup, reporting through
// the handler AgentWebView gives them), every place JusPrin's own code starts
// running by itself -- a CallAfter, a timer, an HTTP callback, a thread --
// through reported() below, and the pages' own top level, which sends a
// report_error message with its Sentry event (WebShared/errorReports.ts). A
// tool call's failure goes to the AI model instead; one in the app's own work
// is also sent through report_if_automatic, when automatic reports are on.
// An exception that leaves any other native event is OrcaSlicer's to handle,
// exactly as upstream handles it (GUI_App::OnExceptionInMainLoop).

#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <utility>

namespace Slic3r::GUI::JusPrin {

// What a report holds: the lines "See what the report contains" lists. The
// event Sentry receives adds what Sentry's SDKs collect (SentryEvent.hpp):
// the stack, the loaded modules, the machine, the recent steps.
struct ErrorReport
{
    std::string app;   // "JusPrin 2.0.0 (OrcaSlicer 2.5.0)"
    std::string os;    // "macOS 26.3"
    std::string doing; // what the app was doing, said by the top handler
    std::string error; // the error as it reached the top, unchanged
    std::string where; // the stack, where one exists (the pages' for now)
    // A page's own Sentry event, as JSON, when a page reported; empty for
    // the app's own.
    std::string page_event;
    // Where the app found its own failure (ThrowStack.hpp failure_stack()),
    // innermost first; empty when it was not taken.
    std::vector<void*> stack;
};

// The AppConfig key of "report errors automatically", off until the person
// turns it on in the dialog or in Preferences › Updates.
inline constexpr const char* kReportErrorsKey = "jusprin_report_errors_automatically";

// Reports one unexpected error: sends it at once when automatic reports are
// on, otherwise opens the dialog after the current event, over the window in
// front. Only top handlers call this.
void report_unexpected_error(std::string doing, std::string error, std::string where = {}, std::string page_event = {});

// Reports a tool call that failed in the app's own work, whose error already
// went to the AI model, or a start report the server answered with a fault
// (LaunchReport): sent only when automatic reports are on, and no dialog
// opens. Any thread.
void report_if_automatic(std::string doing, std::string error);

// The exception being handled, as a report names it: its type and what().
// Call inside a catch block.
std::string describe_current_exception();

// The top handler of an entry point JusPrin's own code starts: wraps the
// body of a CallAfter, a timer, an HTTP callback or a thread, so that an
// exception leaving it is reported, saying the app was `doing` this. Any
// thread.
template<class Body> auto reported(const char* doing, Body body)
{
    return [doing, body = std::move(body)](auto&&... arguments) mutable {
        try {
            body(std::forward<decltype(arguments)>(arguments)...);
        } catch (...) {
            report_unexpected_error(doing, describe_current_exception());
        }
    };
}

# JusPrin's translations

JusPrin shows its interface in the languages OrcaSlicer ships a catalog for
(`languages.json`). OrcaSlicer's own windows translate through upstream's
catalogs in `localization/i18n/`, which the fork never edits. JusPrin's own
text has catalogs of its own here, so taking a new OrcaSlicer release never
conflicts in a translation file:

| Text | Marked with | Catalog | How it reaches the app |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Native windows and text the native side sends to a page | `_L`, `_u8L`, `L`, `_L_CONTEXT`, `_L_PLURAL`, `translated_plural` | `native/<lang>.po`, template `native/JusPrin.pot` | `Brand/brand_catalogs.py` merges it into `resources/i18n/<lang>/<app key>.mo`, the catalog the app loads |
| The AI and Home pages | Lingui macros: `t`, `plural` from `@lingui/core/macro`; `Trans`, `Plural` from `@lingui/react/macro` | `pages/<lang>.po`, one for both pages | `@lingui/vite-plugin` compiles every catalog into each page; the page reads `?lang=` (`AgentUI/src/i18n.tsx`) |

Native text that OrcaSlicer's template already has is left out of
`native/JusPrin.pot`; upstream's translation is used.

## Updating the catalogs

After marking or changing text:

```bash
python3 src/slic3r/GUI/JusPrin/Localization/update_catalogs.py
```

It runs `xgettext` over the native sources and Lingui's extractor over the
pages, then merges the new entries into every language. Translate each new
entry in every `<lang>.po`, following the editing rules in the repository's
`AGENTS.md` (Localization & translations): keep every placeholder and line
break, give each plural form its own inflection, and mark machine
translations `# AI Translated`, adding `# Needs human review: <what>` where
the meaning or the wording is uncertain. `AgentUI/src/catalogs.test.ts` fails
while any entry is untranslated or a translation loses a placeholder.

The page catalogs use ICU placeholders: `{0}`, `{name}`, numbered tags like
`<0>…</0>` for elements inside a `Trans`, and
`{count, plural, one {# printer} other {# printers}}` for counts. Keep the
placeholder and tag names; translate the words, including the words inside a
plural's branches, and give the plural the categories the language uses
(`one`, `few`, `many`, `other`).

